---
import Hero from './Hero.astro';
import Section from './Section.astro';
import AppRow from './AppRow.astro';
import { apps } from '../lib/apps';

// The assembled landing body, shared by the real page (index.astro) and the
// dev-only serif lab (serif.astro) so they can never drift apart.
---

<div class="intro">
  <Hero />
  <!-- Three sentences, three full stops. Not joined with conjunctions (BRIEF §4). -->
  <p class="subhead">
    I'm Todd. I build iOS apps. Flux and Form is my one-person studio in Columbus,
    Ohio.
  </p>
</div>

<Section label="House rules">
  <!--
    One continuous paragraph with bold run-in heads (BRIEF §4). Deliberately NOT
    a list — principles shouldn't share the item rhythm of the Building section.
    Copy is final; set exactly as written.
  -->
  <p class="house-rules">
    <strong>Your data doesn't travel without a reason.</strong> Apps stay on your
    device by default and only reach for a server when they can't work any other
    way. <strong>Nobody's counting.</strong> No streaks, no notifications you
    didn't ask for, nothing measured in time spent.
    <strong>And it has to be worth keeping.</strong> Either it does something
    nothing else does, or it's simply a good time. A slightly worse version of
    something you already have isn't worth either of our time.
  </p>
</Section>

<Section label="Building">
  <div class="building">
    {apps.map((app) => <AppRow app={app} />)}
  </div>
</Section>

<Section label="Background">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Before this, fourteen years in civil design, most recently as a senior
      designer at a national engineering firm here in Columbus. Before that, GIS:
      building the data and the tools other people's work depended on. AWS
      certified, finishing a software development degree.
    </p>
    <p>
      Infrastructure work teaches you to think in loads and tolerances, and to
      assume the thing you're drawing will outlast your involvement in it. I build
      software the same way.
    </p>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .intro {
    margin-bottom: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 2rem);
  }

  .subhead {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    /* Sits on one line at default desktop width; wraps naturally as the
       viewport narrows. Measure widened from its old 34ch so it no longer
       makes an awkward short silhouette under the hero (BRIEF §4 copy intact). */
    font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 0.8rem + 0.85vw, 1.28rem);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
    max-width: var(--measure-text);
    /* Tight to the hero so the two bind as one intro block, well clear of the
       first section rule below. */
    margin-top: clamp(0.6rem, 1.4vw, 1rem);
  }

  /* House rules — one prose paragraph with bold run-in heads. No internal items,
     so no hairline rules. Body size (not the heading step); run-in heads carry
     the same 600 weight the old headings did. */
  .house-rules {
    max-width: var(--measure-text);
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .house-rules strong {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .prose p {
    max-width: var(--measure-text);
  }
  .prose p + p {
    margin-top: 1.15rem;
  }
</style>
